"""The Black Box Problem
Both models can predict with 97% accuracy — but why do they make each prediction? Without understanding, you can't trust the model scientifically, and you can't publish it.
SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) solves this. For every single prediction, SHAP calculates: "How much did each feature contribute to this decision?"
The Beeswarm Plot Explained


```
Feature name │ ← negative impact    |    positive impact →
─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────
checkm_comp  │●●●●● (blue=low) ●●●●●●●●●● (red=high)
```
Each dot = one genome

X position = how strongly that feature pushed the prediction

Left (-) = pushed toward UNCULTURED
Right (+) = pushed toward CULTURED


Colour = the actual feature value for that genome

Red = high value
Blue = low value



Reading checkm_completeness:

Red dots (high completeness) → far right → pushed toward CULTURED ✅ (makes sense!)
Blue dots (low completeness) → far left → pushed toward UNCULTURED ✅ (makes sense!)

This confirms your model learned real biology, not random patterns.

"""

